Cardiomyopathy
Cardiovascular
Exertional dyspnoea, Elevated JVP/Oedema, Gallop rhythm, Structural abnormalities on echo

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-011
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of shortness of breath at rest and progressive severe swelling of the lower legs.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported new-onset orthopnea and significantly worsening exertional dyspnea, signaling impending acute decompensated heart failure. Prompt clinical evaluation was strictly required to ascertain respiratory status, determine immediate need for intravenous diuresis, and assess whether emergency hospital transport was necessary before the next regular working hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't catch my breath when I lie down, and my legs feel so heavy and swollen I can barely lift them."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a gradual decline in exercise tolerance over the past month, now manifesting as severe progressive exertional dyspnea when walking between rooms. Over the last 48 hours, patient has developed orthopnea, requiring three pillows to sleep, and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea. Notes progressive peripheral pitting edema up to the mid-calves. Complains of severe generalized weakness and fatigue. KEY SYMPTOMS: Progressive exertional dyspnoea. Orthopnoea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea. Peripheral pitting oedema. Fatigue and severe generalized weakness.
Functional Status: Now largely chair-bound due to dyspnea and fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Mobility severely limited, spouse struggling to assist with basic transfers.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 22 | SpO2: 93 percent | Wt: 88 Kg (up 4kg from baseline)
General: Visibly dyspneic while talking, sitting upright in chair, peripherally warm.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Jugular venous pressure elevated to 5 cm above sternal angle. Laterally displaced, diffuse apex beat. Tachycardic regular rhythm with a distinct third heart sound (S3) gallop. Extremities: Bilateral pitting edema extending to the knees, non-tender. Respiratory: Fine bibasal inspiratory crackles extending one-third up the lung fields. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Exertional dyspnoea, Elevated JVP/Oedema, Gallop rhythm, Structural abnormalities on echo (pending formal echocardiogram).
Environmental Observations: Patient using recliner chair to sleep due to orthopnea.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with prescribed bisoprolol and low-dose ramipril. Denies taking extra fluid tablets.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cardiomyopathy (Decompensated Heart Failure) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, bilateral nature of edema and the presence of systemic fluid overload signs (elevated JVP, pulmonary crackles).

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual onset of progressive congestive symptoms over weeks rather than sudden onset pleuritic pain and acute desaturation.

Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of wheeze, productive purulent cough, and presence of an S3 gallop and elevated JVP.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits classic signs of acutely decompensated heart failure secondary to an underlying cardiomyopathy, evidenced by severe orthopnea, elevated JVP, bilateral peripheral edema, and an S3 gallop. The recent rapid weight gain and increasing respiratory distress indicate profound volume overload. Assessment could not wait until routine hours as the patient's respiratory status was actively declining, posing an imminent risk of acute pulmonary edema and hypoxic respiratory failure requiring emergency intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral furosemide 40mg immediately to initiate diuresis. Arranged urgent outpatient transthoracic echocardiogram and basic metabolic panel (renal function, electrolytes) for the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to implement strict daily weight monitoring every morning after voiding and before breakfast. By linking weight fluctuations directly to fluid retention, the patient learns early recognition of decompensation. The reward is maintaining control over their symptoms and avoiding future emergency hospital admissions through proactive dose adjustment.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: Continue sleeping in recliner tonight to optimize respiratory mechanics.
Follow-up: Urgent General Practice review within 24 hours to review diuresis response and titrate heart failure medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If breathing becomes extremely labored at rest, if the patient develops chest pain, or if confusion occurs, an ambulance must be called immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal echocardiography and laboratory confirmation; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Heart failure exacerbations require aggressive fluid offloading. Ongoing management requires close medication titration and fluid/salt restriction.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Stay seated upright. Restrict fluid intake to 1.5 liters in the next 24 hours. Seek immediate emergency care if breathing severely worsens.

